-- title:
--   Irreducible cuspidal piece generated by smooth Casimir eigenvectors
-- statement:
--   Fix a homomorphism $\omega$ from the ideles of $\mathbb{Q}$ to $\mathbb{C}^\times$ with $|\omega(z)|=1$ for all $z$, reals $a,b$ and a set $\Phi_0\subseteq \mathrm{GL}_3(\mathbb{A}_\mathbb{Q})$ with `IsSlabDomain a b Φ₀`, i.e. $0<a<b$ and $\Phi_0$ is a fundamental domain for the image of the global points $\mathrm{GL}_3(\mathbb{Q})$ with respect to the slab measure attached to $a,b$. Let $V$ be a $\mathbb{C}$-submodule of the carrier $L^2(\mathbb{C},\mu_{a,b,\Phi_0})$. Call $F$ a cusp function when it lies in the automorphic submodule for $\omega,a,b,\Phi_0$, is continuous, and is cuspidal along the unipotent radicals of the $(2,1)$- and $(1,2)$-parabolics for the designated carrier pins; write $[F]$ for the image of such an $F$ under `toL2`. Assume: $V$ is the topological closure of the span of the classes $[F]$ of cusp functions with $[F]\in V$; $V\neq\bot$; whenever $[F]\in V$ for a cusp function $F$, also $[F(\cdot\, g)]\in V$ for every $g$ such that the translate is a cusp function, and $[\varphi*F]\in V$ for every smoothing kernel $\varphi$ (a smooth archimedean factor times the indicator of a product of compact open subgroups, almost all maximal) whose smoothing $x\mapsto\int \varphi(g)F(xg)\,dg$ is a cusp function; and $V$ is minimal, i.e. every submodule $W\le V$ enjoying these same three closure and stability properties is $\bot$ or $V$. Fix $c_1,c_2,c_3\in\mathbb{C}$ and a cusp function $G$ with $[G]\in V$, $[G]\neq 0$, satisfying `IsArchSmooth3` (smoothness in the archimedean matrix entries on the invertible locus), such that every iterated archimedean derivative $\mathrm{archDeriv}\,i_1j_1\cdots$ of $G$ along a list of pairs in $\mathrm{Fin}\,3\times\mathrm{Fin}\,3$ is continuous, and such that $\Omega_1G=c_1G$, $\Omega_2G=c_2G$, $\Omega_3G=c_3G$, where $\Omega_1=\sum_i D_{ii}$, $\Omega_2=\sum_{i,j}D_{ij}D_{ji}$, $\Omega_3=\sum_{i,j,k}D_{ij}D_{jk}D_{ki}$ with $D_{ij}$ the derivative at $s=0$ of the right translate by the archimedean lift of $1+sE_{ij}$. Then $V$ is the topological closure of the span of the classes $[f]$ of those cusp functions $f$ with $[f]\in V$ that are archimedean-smooth, have all iterated archimedean derivatives continuous, and satisfy $\Omega_1f=c_1f$, $\Omega_2f=c_2f$, $\Omega_3f=c_3f$.
--
--   This is the step in the proof that the centre of the enveloping algebra of $\mathfrak{gl}_3$ acts by scalars on an irreducible piece of the cuspidal $L^2$-spectrum of $\mathrm{GL}_3(\mathbb{A}_\mathbb{Q})$: from a single non-zero smooth eigenvector with eigenvalues $c_1,c_2,c_3$ one recovers all of $V$ as the closed span of such eigenvectors, the point being that the three central operators commute with right translation and with smoothing, so that the closed span of the eigenvectors inherits the stability properties and minimality applies. It is used by [`LanglandsTunnell.CubicInduction.SlabL2.exists_casimir_eq_smul_of_irreducible_cuspidal`](thm.html#LanglandsTunnell.CubicInduction.SlabL2.exists_casimir_eq_smul_of_irreducible_cuspidal).
